==Irish==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|ga|baoghal||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ga|sga|báegul||danger}}<ref>{{R:DIL|5134|head=báegul}}</ref> (compare {{cog|gd|baoghal||harm, peril}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Munster}} {{IPA|ga|/bˠeːl̪ˠ/|ref={{R:ga:SjPh|79}}}}
* {{a|Connacht}} {{IPA|ga|/bˠiːlˠ/|/bˠiːl̪ˠ/}}
* {{a|Ulster}} {{IPA|ga|/bˠiːlˠ/|/bˠiːl̪ˠ/|/bˠɯːlˠ/|qual3=older}}

{{collapse|title=Comments|bg=lightgray
|The genitive singular of this word is pronounced {{IPAchar|/bˠeːlʲ/}} in {{w|Munster}} and formerly {{w|County Kilkenny}}, and not *{{IPAchar|/bˠiːlʲ/}} as wrongly suggested by the {{w|Caighdeán Oifigiúil}}’s reduced spelling ''baoil''. In Munster and formerly County Kilkenny, ''ao'' {{IPAchar|/ɯː/}} before "broad" (non-palatal) consonants has become {{IPAchar|/eː/}}, whereas ''ao'' {{IPAchar|/ɯː/}} before "slender" (palatal) consonants has become {{IPAchar|/iː/}}. Accordingly, in these areas both the nominative singular ''baoghal'' {{IPAchar|/bˠɯːɣəlˠ/}} and genitive singular ''baoghail'' {{IPAchar|/bˠɯːɣəlʲ/}} developed the vowel {{IPAchar|/eː/}}, which remained after the loss of intervocalic {{IPAchar|/ɣ/}}. For a similar pronunciation mismatch in the current standard spelling, compare the reduced spelling of {{m|ga|saoil}} from Classical {{m|ga|saoghail}}.}}

